**Basic happy-path assertions**
- `expectNextMatches(predicate)` for value checks
- `expectNextCount(n)` when counts matter more than values
- Always call `.verify(Duration)` to bound CI time

```java
@Test
void findByIdReturnsOrder() {
    StepVerifier.create(orderService.findById("abc"))
        .expectNextMatches(o -> o.id().equals("abc"))
        .expectComplete()
        .verify(Duration.ofSeconds(2));
}

@Test
void listReturnsTenItems() {
    StepVerifier.create(orderService.list())
        .expectNextCount(10)
        .verifyComplete();
}
```

**Error assertions**
- Either match by predicate or by class

```java
@Test
void missingOrderEmitsNotFound() {
    StepVerifier.create(orderService.findById("missing"))
        .expectErrorMatches(t -> t instanceof OrderNotFound
            && t.getMessage().contains("missing"))
        .verify();
}

.expectError(OrderNotFound.class);
```

**Backpressure-controlled tests**
- `initialRequest(0)` means the subscriber requests nothing at first
- `thenRequest(n)` advances demand; reveals operator behavior under controlled pressure

```java
@Test
void backpressureRespected() {
    StepVerifier.create(
            orderService.streamAll(),
            StepVerifierOptions.create().initialRequest(0))
        .expectSubscription()
        .thenRequest(2)
        .expectNextCount(2)
        .thenRequest(3)
        .expectNextCount(3)
        .thenCancel()
        .verify();
}
```

**`TestPublisher` for controlled input**
- Use when you want to drive specific sequences (next, error, complete) into code under test

```java
@Test
void processingHandlesError() {
    TestPublisher<Event> source = TestPublisher.create();
    Flux<Result> results = processor.process(source.flux());

    StepVerifier.create(results)
        .then(() -> source.next(new Event("ok")))
        .expectNextMatches(r -> r.status() == OK)
        .then(() -> source.error(new RuntimeException("boom")))
        .expectErrorMessage("boom")
        .verify();
}
```

**Specific assertions per element**
- `assertNext` runs an arbitrary block on one element
- `thenConsumeWhile(predicate)` skips elements matching a predicate

```java
.assertNext(order -> assertThat(order.amount()).isEqualByComparingTo("99.99"))
.thenConsumeWhile(o -> o.status().equals("PAID"))
.expectComplete()
.verify();
```

**The `.verify()` rule**
- Without `.verify()` / `.verifyComplete()` / `.verify(Duration)` the test passes trivially because nothing subscribed
- Always set a Duration bound to keep CI fast

**Slice test with `@WebFluxTest`**
- Boots only the WebFlux infrastructure plus the listed controller
- `@MockitoBean` mocks downstream services

```java
@WebFluxTest(OrderController.class)
@Import(ErrorConfig.class)
class OrderControllerTest {

    @Autowired WebTestClient client;

    @MockitoBean OrderService service;

    @Test
    void get200() {
        when(service.findById("abc"))
            .thenReturn(Mono.just(new OrderDto("abc", "PAID", BigDecimal.TEN)));

        client.get().uri("/orders/abc")
            .exchange()
            .expectStatus().isOk()
            .expectBody(OrderDto.class)
            .value(o -> assertThat(o.id()).isEqualTo("abc"));
    }

    @Test
    void get404() {
        when(service.findById("missing"))
            .thenReturn(Mono.error(new OrderNotFound("missing")));

        client.get().uri("/orders/missing")
            .exchange()
            .expectStatus().isNotFound();
    }

    @Test
    void validationFailure() {
        client.post().uri("/orders")
            .contentType(APPLICATION_JSON)
            .bodyValue("{\"amount\": -1}")
            .exchange()
            .expectStatus().isBadRequest()
            .expectBody(ProblemDetail.class)
            .value(pd -> assertThat(pd.getDetail()).contains("must be positive"));
    }
}
```

**Streaming response (SSE)**
- `returnResult` plus a `ParameterizedTypeReference` extracts the streamed body
- `StepVerifier` then asserts on the `Flux`

```java
@Test
void streamsSSE() {
    Flux<ServerSentEvent<OrderEvent>> responseBody = client.get().uri("/orders/events")
        .accept(MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM)
        .exchange()
        .expectStatus().isOk()
        .returnResult(new ParameterizedTypeReference<ServerSentEvent<OrderEvent>>() {})
        .getResponseBody();

    StepVerifier.create(responseBody)
        .expectNextCount(3)
        .thenCancel()
        .verify(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
}
```

**Full integration test with Testcontainers**
- `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)` boots the real app
- `@DynamicPropertySource` wires Testcontainers URLs into Spring properties

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)
@Testcontainers
class OrderIT {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> pg = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void registerPgProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry reg) {
        reg.add("spring.r2dbc.url", () ->
            "r2dbc:postgresql://%s:%d/%s".formatted(
                pg.getHost(), pg.getMappedPort(5432), pg.getDatabaseName()));
        reg.add("spring.r2dbc.username", pg::getUsername);
        reg.add("spring.r2dbc.password", pg::getPassword);
        reg.add("spring.flyway.url", pg::getJdbcUrl);
        reg.add("spring.flyway.user", pg::getUsername);
        reg.add("spring.flyway.password", pg::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired WebTestClient client;

    @Test
    void createAndFetch() {
        OrderDto created = client.post().uri("/orders")
            .bodyValue(new CreateOrder("cust-1", BigDecimal.TEN))
            .exchange()
            .expectStatus().isCreated()
            .expectBody(OrderDto.class)
            .returnResult().getResponseBody();

        client.get().uri("/orders/" + created.id())
            .exchange()
            .expectStatus().isOk()
            .expectBody(OrderDto.class)
            .value(o -> assertThat(o.amount()).isEqualByComparingTo("10"));
    }
}
```

**MockWebServer (okhttp) for `WebClient` tests**
- Lighter-weight than WireMock for simple cases
- Set up before each test, shut down after

```java
class PaymentClientTest {
    private MockWebServer server;
    private PaymentClient client;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() throws IOException {
        server = new MockWebServer();
        server.start();
        WebClient wc = WebClient.builder()
            .baseUrl(server.url("/").toString())
            .build();
        client = new PaymentClient(wc);
    }

    @AfterEach
    void tearDown() throws IOException { server.shutdown(); }

    @Test
    void retriesOn503ThenSucceeds() {
        server.enqueue(new MockResponse().setResponseCode(503));
        server.enqueue(new MockResponse().setResponseCode(503));
        server.enqueue(new MockResponse()
            .setResponseCode(200)
            .setBody("{\"status\": \"CAPTURED\"}")
            .addHeader("Content-Type", "application/json"));

        StepVerifier.create(client.charge(new Charge("ord-1", BigDecimal.TEN)))
            .expectNextMatches(r -> r.status() == CAPTURED)
            .verifyComplete();

        assertThat(server.getRequestCount()).isEqualTo(3);
    }

    @Test
    void propagatesTimeout() {
        server.enqueue(new MockResponse()
            .setBody("{}")
            .setBodyDelay(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS));

        StepVerifier.create(client.charge(new Charge("ord-1", BigDecimal.TEN)))
            .expectError(TimeoutException.class)
            .verify(Duration.ofSeconds(6));
    }
}
```

**WireMock** is the other popular option — richer features (request matching, recording), heavier setup.

**Test `Flux.interval`**
- Verify nothing emits before the first 100 ms
- Advance the clock and consume the next two emissions

```java
@Test
void intervalEmitsEvery100ms() {
    StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(() ->
            Flux.interval(Duration.ofMillis(100)).take(3))
        .expectSubscription()
        .expectNoEvent(Duration.ofMillis(100))
        .expectNext(0L)
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofMillis(200))
        .expectNext(1L, 2L)
        .verifyComplete();
}
```

**Test `Mono.delay`**

```java
@Test
void monoDelayFiresAfterDelay() {
    StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(() ->
            Mono.delay(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).map(i -> "done"))
        .expectSubscription()
        .expectNoEvent(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .expectNext("done")
        .verifyComplete();
}
```

**Test `delayElements`**

```java
@Test
void delayElementsBetweenItems() {
    StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(() ->
            Flux.just("a", "b", "c").delayElements(Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
        .expectSubscription()
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofSeconds(1)).expectNext("a")
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofSeconds(1)).expectNext("b")
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofSeconds(1)).expectNext("c")
        .verifyComplete();
}
```

**Test `timeout` triggers correctly**

```java
@Test
void timeoutFiresWhenSlow() {
    StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(() ->
            Mono.delay(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
                .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2)))
        .expectSubscription()
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
        .expectError(TimeoutException.class)
        .verify();
}
```

**Test `Retry.backoff`**
- Each retry waits an increasing delay; virtual time lets you assert exhaustion in milliseconds

```java
@Test
void retryWithBackoffExhausts() {
    AtomicInteger calls = new AtomicInteger();

    StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(() ->
            Mono.fromCallable(() -> {
                    calls.incrementAndGet();
                    throw new RuntimeException("fail");
                })
                .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofSeconds(1))))
        .expectSubscription()
        .thenAwait(Duration.ofMinutes(1))
        .expectError()
        .verify();

    assertThat(calls.get()).isEqualTo(4);
}
```

**Custom `VirtualTimeScheduler` with options**
- Useful when you want a specific scheduler instance

```java
VirtualTimeScheduler scheduler = VirtualTimeScheduler.create();

StepVerifier.withVirtualTime(
        () -> Flux.interval(Duration.ofSeconds(1), scheduler).take(3),
        () -> scheduler,
        Long.MAX_VALUE)
    .expectSubscription()
    .thenAwait(Duration.ofSeconds(3))
    .expectNextCount(3)
    .verifyComplete();
```

**Replacing Reactor schedulers globally for a test**
- Heavier hammer; rarely needed if you build inside the supplier

```java
@BeforeEach
void useVirtualScheduler() {
    VirtualTimeScheduler.set(VirtualTimeScheduler.create());
}

@AfterEach
void resetSchedulers() {
    VirtualTimeScheduler.reset();
}
```

This swaps every Reactor scheduler with the virtual one. Use sparingly — it can hide bugs that depend on real scheduler behavior.
